  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nCurrently, west/northwest flow aloft with broad upper level\nridging over the Northern Rockies and a weak southerly return\nflow over the Northern Plains. \n\nBroad upper level ridge migrates east into the Northern Plains\ntomorrow, as a deep upper level low develops into southwestern\nCanada. Lee side surface trough deepens across the Northern \nHigh Plains as a result, increasing southerly return flow and \nWAA into the region. Afternoon highs tomorrow will warm well \ninto the 70s to low/mid 80s, well above our normal values in \nthe lower 60s. \n\nSouthwest flow aloft develops on Saturday, pushing the surface \ntrough east into the central Dakotas during the day. This \nallows CAA on the backside of the frontal boundary but remains\nwarm along and east (highs Sat from low 60s northwest to low \n80s southeast). Embedded lead waves originating from a deepening\nwest coast trough overspread the region Sat night into Sunday, \nwith the sfc trough/frontal boundary slowly moving to the \nsoutheast. This allows CAA to overspend all of western and \ncentral North Dakota, with high temperatures Sun aft ranging \nfrom the mid 40s northwest to lower 60s southeast. Precipitation\nchances start to increase via NBM/ensembles Sat night/Sun, and \nespecially Sunday night through Monday when upper level jet \nsupport (left exit region) increases, favorable for widespread \nprecipitation. CAA and falling sfc temperatures may also result\nin some snow possibly mixing in with rain Sun night/Mon AM, \nthough little if any accumulation expected. \n\nSplit flow aloft early next week will maintain below normal high\ntemperatures in the 40s (Mon) and 50s (Tue) along with mainly\ndry weather. Widespread near to below freezing temperatures also\nforecast both Sunday and Monday nights, with Monday night/Tue\nmorning looking like the better chance for a freeze and\nassociated headlines. \n\nGEFS/EC ensemble means favor a southern stream low/S/WV trough\nlifting through central Rockies and into the Great Plain mid to\nlate next week, and would suggest a moderation in temperatures\nas well as increasing precipitation chances for the Dakotas.",
